Use FTP from the Ubuntu VM to fetch the files, then initialize a Git repo and push it to GitHub.

Best practice: use SFTP instead of plain FTP if possible, because FTP is insecure. But if you specifically need FTP, this works.

1) Install the tools
```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y ftp git
```

2) Connect to the remote FTP server from the Ubuntu VM
Example:
```bash
ftp ftp.example.com
```

Then log in:
```bash
username
password
```

Transfer files to the VM:
```bash
cd /path/on/ftp/server
lcd /home/ubuntu/project
mget *
```

If you want to upload files from the VM to the FTP server:
```bash
cd /path/on/ftp/server
put /home/ubuntu/project/file.txt
mput /home/ubuntu/project/*
```

Exit FTP:
```bash
bye
```

3) Go to the project folder and initialize Git
```bash
cd /home/ubuntu/project
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
```

4) Create the GitHub repository
On GitHub:
- Click New repository
- Name it
- Do not initialize with README if you already have files
- Create repository

5) Connect the local repo to GitHub
```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO.git
git push -u origin main
```

If you used GitHub CLI instead:
```bash
gh auth login
gh repo create YOUR_REPO --public --source=. --remote=origin --push
```

If you already initialized Git and want to add origin later:
```bash
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO.git
git push -u origin main
```

If you want a more modern secure version, use SFTP instead of FTP:
```bash
sudo apt install -y openssh-client
sftp username@ftp.example.com
```

Then:
```bash
cd /remote/path
lcd /home/ubuntu/project
get -r *
```

If you want, I can give you the exact command sequence for your specific FTP server details (host, username, password, remote folder, and GitHub repo URL).
